<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Venetian Negroponte</h1><p class="st-meta">1390 CE – 1470 CE · capital Negroponte</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Venetian-ruled Euboea, a key Aegean naval base whose fall to Mehmed II in 1470 was one of the great blows to Venice's empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> had long dominated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/triarchy-of-negroponte">Triarchy of Negroponte</a> through its bailo in the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a> (<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a>). As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> triarchs weakened in the face of the Turks and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-company">Catalans</a>, Venice took over their lands, and by 1390 ruled the whole island directly.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A Venetian bailo and councillors governed from the fortified town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripus-strait">Euripus</a> strait, where a bridge linked the island to the mainland. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a> was a major port of call for Venetian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>, a grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> market and a base for watching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. A Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> majority lived under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> elite.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1470 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a> by land and sea and stormed it in July; the bailo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-erizzo">Paolo Erizzo</a> was killed. The loss shocked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and left Euboea Ottoman until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label=